{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.85,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["Q1", "Q2", "R1", "R2", "R5", "U2", "U3", "J2"],
      "pins": ["Q2.1", "Q1.1", "U2.6", "U3.6", "J2.4", "J2.2"],
      "nets": ["PWM", "/PWM_OUT", "/VCC_OUT", "VCC"],
      "observed": "The net 'PWM' connects U2.6 (PB1), U3.6 (PB1), R2.1 (base resistor to Q2), and R5.2 (series resistor to fan PWM output). Q2 drives Q1's gate, and Q1 switches VCC to VCC_OUT (fan power). Thus the same PWM signal both drives the fan's PWM input and switches the fan's +12V power supply.",
      "expected": "For a 4-wire fan, the fan's power should be continuously on (or controlled by a separate enable signal), and only the PWM wire should carry the PWM control signal. Q2's base should not be connected to the PWM net.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"10\") (name \"PWM\") ... (node (ref \"U2\") (pin \"6\") ...) (node (ref \"R2\") (pin \"1\") ...) (node (ref \"R5\") (pin \"2\") ...))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"4\") (name \"/VCC_OUT\") ... (node (ref \"J2\") (pin \"2\") ...) (node (ref \"Q1\") (pin \"3\") ...))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"15\") (name \"VCC\") ... (node (ref \"Q1\") (pin \"2\") ...) (node (ref \"R1\") (pin \"1\") ...))"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The fan's power supply is chopped at the PWM frequency. A 4-wire fan's internal controller will lose power every PWM cycle, causing malfunction, no tach output, or no operation.",
      "confidence": 0.9
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["C2", "U1"],
      "pins": ["C2.1", "U1.2"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "GND"],
      "observed": "C2 is a 100nF capacitor on the +5V output of the LM1117-5.0 regulator (U1.2).",
      "expected": "The LM1117-5.0 datasheet requires a minimum output capacitor of 10µF (tantalum or aluminum electrolytic) for stability. 100nF is far too small.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(comp (ref \"C2\") (value \"100nF\") (footprint \"Capacitor_SMD:C_0805_2012Metric\") ...)"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(node (ref \"C2\") (pin \"1\") (pintype \"passive\")) in (net (code \"1\") (name \"+5V\") ... (node (ref \"U1\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"VO\") ...))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(comp (ref \"U1\") (value \"LM1117-5.0\") (datasheet \"http://www.ti.com/lit/ds/symlink/lm1117.pdf\") ...)"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The 5V regulator may oscillate or be unstable, causing a noisy or incorrect 5V rail that can affect the microcontrollers and analog pot reading.",
      "confidence": 0.8
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design has two significant issues: the fan's power supply is switched by the PWM signal (via Q1/Q2), which will cause a 4-wire fan to lose power on every PWM cycle, and the LM1117-5.0 output capacitor is only 100nF instead of the required ≥10µF, risking regulator instability."
}

